  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework's performance and emission reduction metrics are based on experimental simulations rather than a real-world city deployment.",
      "what": "The study develops a two-phase optimization framework that first solves the Charging Location and Electric Vehicle Scheduling Problem to create feasible electric bus schedules, and then uses these schedules in a multi-period transition planning model to minimize total cost of ownership. The model determines fleet replacement and charging infrastructure deployment over time, achieving up to an 85% reduction in local emissions compared to a diesel-only baseline.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that planning electric bus transitions using vehicle schedules specifically optimized for electric operations, rather than relying on legacy diesel schedules, significantly lowers the total cost of ownership and accelerates emission reductions by prioritizing long-distance routes with sufficient idle time."
